  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of rolls until a $6$ came on. The probability of getting a $6$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of rolls until a $6$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{6}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a $6$ on the $2$nd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2-1}(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & \u22480.139,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
